<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=arpanet">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">ARPANET</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1969">1969</a>–<a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Advanced Research Projects Agency Network</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The ARPANET (Advanced Research Projects Agency Network) was the first wide-area packet-switched network with distributed control, set up by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Defense; its first host-to-host connection, between UCLA and the Stanford Research Institute, was made on 29 October 1969. Bob Taylor began the project in 1966 to let researchers share remote computers, and Larry Roberts adopted Donald Davies' concepts for packet switching. In 1973 three-quarters of its traffic was email; it adopted TCP/IP in 1983 and was decommissioned in 1990, having laid the technical foundation of the Internet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arpanet.jpg" alt="ARPANET" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles S. Kline · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First-arpanet-imp-log.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1969</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>California</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Bob Taylor · Larry Roberts · <a href="/empire/united-states">United States</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Its packet switching drew on Donald Davies’ work at Britain’s National Physical Laboratory, whose network came first.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/eniac">ENIAC<span>1945</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/world-wide-web">World Wide Web<span>1989</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=arpanet">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
